UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 1: Aural skills and listening', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops. Pitch is how high or low a sound sits, and duration is how long that sound or gap lasts. A classroom can teach both with no recording: claps are short and hard to hum back, while a hummed note has one pitch you can match. The pause between them is not a mistake. Its duration changes when the next sound arrives. You listen for pitch, duration, and silence together so a description could be performed again from words alone.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou hear two claps, a short silence, one foot stamp, and then a hum that sits higher than a comfortable speaking voice. How do pitch and duration differ for each event, including the silence?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence with its own length. The two claps are short and not one clear pitch. The stamp is also short and unpitched. The silence has duration but no pitch. The final hum is higher than speaking level and lasts longer than a clap. Naming only \"some noises\" leaves out the features a listener needs.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSilence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises. That fails because a gap has duration and it moves the next sound later. If you delete the pause, the hum seems to answer the claps at once, which is a different piece. Put the silence back in the description and give it a count.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nTap a spoon three times on a table, leave one count of silence, then hum a low note for two counts. On paper, list pitch and duration for the taps, the silence, and the low hum. Do not reuse the four-clap pattern or the higher hum from the practice. A parent can listen once and tick whether the silence was given a length.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Music","learning_intention":"Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops. Pitch is how high or low a sound sits, and duration is how long that sound or gap lasts. A classroom can teach both with no recording: claps are short and hard to hum back, while a hummed note has one pitch you can match. The pause between them is not a mistake. Its duration changes when the next sound arrives. You listen for pitch, duration, and silence together so a description could be performed again from words alone.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MU4-LIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Tap a spoon three times on a table, leave one count of silence, then hum a low note for two counts. On paper, list pitch and duration for the taps, the silence, and the low hum. Do not reuse the four-clap pattern or the  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using pitch on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises.. Correction: A strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises. That fails because a gap has duration and it moves the next sound later. If you delete the pause, the hum seems to answer the claps at once, which is a different piece. Put the silence back in the description and give it a count. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating pitch on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with pitch, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about pitch with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for pitch. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for pitch. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","A strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence","Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises"],"question":"You hear two claps, a short silence, one foot stamp, and then a hum that sits higher than a comfortable speaking voice. How do pitch and duration differ for each event, including the silence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for pitch; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises","Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops","A strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence","Careful use of silence with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named silence, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name silence only, without linking it to pitch","Apply pitch to new material and name the silence used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises"],"question":"Which response best applies pitch with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on pitch. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for pitch"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Four even claps, a two-count silent pause, then one medium hum held for three counts"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises","Name silence only, with no linked example","Tap a spoon three times on a table, leave one count of silence, then hum a low note for two counts"],"question":"Which independent response best applies pitch to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about pitch with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating pitch on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using pitch on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for pitch. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with pitch, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises.. Correction: A strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-music-y9-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Music 7–10 (2024) outcome MU4-LIS-01. Content-point mapping is pending. Not NESA verified. No copyrighted recordings are required; examples are original descriptions or student-made sounds.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise pitch.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use pitch with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify silence and explain why it matters.","I can practise duration independently on a new prompt.","I can notice listening needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFour even claps, a two-count silent pause, then one medium hum held for three counts. Each clap is a short duration and has no single pitch to copy with the voice. The pause is silence with a duration of about two walking counts, so you do not rush into the hum. The hum has a clear medium pitch and a longer duration than any clap. Write those three facts, including the silence, before you add any story about the sound.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou hear two claps, a short silence, one foot stamp, and then a hum that sits higher than a comfortable speaking voice. How do pitch and duration differ for each event, including the silence?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence with its own length. The two claps are short and not one clear pitch. The stamp is also short and unpitched. The silence has duration but no pitch. The final hum is higher than speaking level and lasts longer than a clap. Naming only \"some noises\" leaves out the features a listener needs.","explain":"Sound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops. Pitch is how high or low a sound sits, and duration is how long that sound or gap lasts. A classroom can teach both with no recording: claps are short and hard to hum back, while a hummed note has one pitch you can match. The pause between them is not a mistake. Its duration changes when the next sound arrives. You listen for pitch, duration, and silence together so a description could be performed again from words alone.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Silence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises. That fails because a gap has duration and it moves the next sound later. If you delete the pause, the hum seems to answer the claps at once, which is a different piece. Put the silence back in the description and give it a count. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a heard clap or hum with a timed silent gap.","caption":"Silence is a duration, not a skipped blank.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sound</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Clap or hum you can hear</text><text x=\"358\