nd has no single pitch to copy with the voice. The pause is silence with a duration of about two walking counts, so you do not rush into the hum. The hum has a clear medium pitch and a longer duration than any clap. Write those three facts, including the silence, before you add any story about the sound. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast pitch of a hum with the duration of a sound.","caption":"Name pitch and duration as different facts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pitch</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How high or low the hum sits</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Duration</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">How long the sound lasts</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSilence is empty waiting, so a careful listener should skip every gap and describe only the noises. That fails because a gap has duration and it moves the next sound later. If you delete the pause, the hum seems to answer the claps at once, which is a different piece. Put the silence back in the description and give it a count. Here is a more helpful example: Four even claps, a two-count silent pause, then one medium hum held for three counts. Each clap is a short duration and has no single pitch to copy with the voice. The pause is silence with a duration of about two walking counts, so you do not rush into the hum. The hum has a clear medium pitch and a longer duration than any clap. Write those three facts, including the silence, before you add any story about the sound.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer gives every event a duration and says whether it has a hummable pitch, and it treats the gap as silence with 