ith no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pitch?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSound is anything you can hear, and silence is the timed gap where hearing stops. Pitch is how high or low a sound sits, and duration is how long that sound or gap lasts. A classroom can teach both with no recording: claps are short and hard to hum back, while a hummed note has one pitch you can match. The pause between them is not a mistake. Its duration changes when the next sound arrives. You listen for pitch, duration, and silence together so a description could be performed again from words alone. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 1: Aural skills and listening","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Aural skills and listening","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nFour even claps, a two-count silent pause, then one medium hum held for three counts. Each clap is a short duration and has no single pitch to copy with the voice. The pause is silence with a duration of about two walking counts, so you do not rush into the hum. The hum has a clear medium pitch and a longer duration than any clap. Write those three facts, including the silence, before you add any story about the sound.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a heard clap or hum with a timed silent gap.","caption":"Silence is a duration, not a skipped blank.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sound</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Clap or hum you can hear</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Silence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Timed gap with no sound</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two boxes contrast pitch of a hum with the duration of a sound.","caption":"Name pitch and duration as different facts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e